00:17:37.175 --> 00:17:41.212
So if you have a chance to
go to a program like this,

00:17:41.212 --> 00:17:45.936
spend the time work in Silicon Valley or
other companies.

00:17:45.936 --> 00:17:49.623
It's something that it's super
valuable for you coming back and

00:17:49.623 --> 00:17:52.582
wanting to join a company
in Brazil that or in Latin.

00:17:52.582 --> 00:17:57.762
That usually can't have access
to talent that actually was

00:17:57.762 --> 00:18:03.260
exposed to doing things like
companies in the US have, right?

00:18:03.260 --> 00:18:07.233
You hire an Engineer there,
they spent a few years on one, two,

00:18:07.233 --> 00:18:10.116
three companies that
you're aspiring to be.

00:18:10.116 --> 00:18:12.354
Here, that's much harder and

00:18:12.354 --> 00:18:16.321
easier to find with people
that are in these networks.

00:18:20.785 --> 00:18:25.350
&gt;&gt; And Marco,
since you've been at GSB the longest,

00:18:25.350 --> 00:18:31.733
if you had to do the MBA again,
what would you do differently this time?

00:18:35.968 --> 00:18:40.493
&gt;&gt; Well, that's a good question.

00:18:40.493 --> 00:18:46.196
I think when I did my my MBA,
I focus most of my efforts in attending

00:18:46.196 --> 00:18:51.491
lectures by guest speakers and
building relationships with

00:18:51.491 --> 00:18:56.703
my classmates, and both on and
outside of the golf course.

00:18:56.703 --> 00:18:58.782
And I think that's what I would do again.

00:18:58.782 --> 00:19:02.782
I think that was the most valuable
things for me were those,

00:19:02.782 --> 00:19:07.836
just getting inspired by the amazing
lecture speakers that we would have.

00:19:07.836 --> 00:19:12.683
Again, one of those ended
up investing in my company.

00:19:12.683 --> 00:19:21.348
And the incredible classmates I had who
basically helped me build my business.

00:19:21.348 --> 00:19:26.526
But are also many others that are not
working, my colleague, lifelong friends

00:19:26.526 --> 00:19:31.412
that are two different things and
are just an amazing community to be with.

00:19:31.412 --> 00:19:36.935
So I think that's one of the most
valuable things that GSB provides.

00:19:36.935 --> 00:19:42.016
I didn't get into more
technical classes at the time.

00:19:42.016 --> 00:19:46.175
I think I would these days,
I would do some programming.

00:19:46.175 --> 00:19:50.168
I would learn some more code,
something I didn't do then.

00:19:50.168 --> 00:19:55.881
I would definitely, rather than
learning the basic accounting skills or

00:19:55.881 --> 00:20:02.158
maybe basic statistics skills, I would
definitely learn basic coding skills.

00:20:05.682 --> 00:20:07.408
&gt;&gt; David?

00:20:07.408 --> 00:20:11.536
&gt;&gt; Not much to add to be honest, I think,

00:20:11.536 --> 00:20:16.438
I was happy to be able to
have spent a lot of time

00:20:16.438 --> 00:20:21.480
with friends and
meeting really good people.

00:20:21.480 --> 00:20:24.552
Expanding the horizon with
the job opportunities,

00:20:24.552 --> 00:20:28.543
having been able to work through my
second year, get me up to speed.

00:20:28.543 --> 00:20:31.612
I had the opportunity to start spending
time in looking at investing in

00:20:31.612 --> 00:20:32.347
Latin America.

00:20:32.347 --> 00:20:35.691
And to me, that was what I wanted to do.

00:20:35.691 --> 00:20:38.348
I want to be sure I wanted
to go back to Latin America.

00:20:38.348 --> 00:20:42.680
One of the advices that I
received from mentor was,

00:20:42.680 --> 00:20:45.602
I think this is helpful perhaps for

00:20:45.602 --> 00:20:50.459
everybody in the panels which
are on the community here.

00:20:50.459 --> 00:20:53.006
Which is strategically,
you want to position

00:20:53.006 --> 00:20:57.035
yourself where there is a scarcity and
know where there is oversupply.

00:20:57.035 --> 00:21:01.917
Now I always thought that a Latin American
that went to US and stayed in the US

00:21:01.917 --> 00:21:06.042
had no real differentiation,
was going where everybody was.

00:21:06.042 --> 00:21:10.940
But a Latin American that could go to
the US and then go back to Latin America.

00:21:10.940 --> 00:21:14.782
That's where there was real scarcity and
continues to be a scarcity.

00:21:14.782 --> 00:21:19.346
And so I think that's ultimately very
related to the impact that you can

00:21:19.346 --> 00:21:22.551
create in some of the countries
that we come from.

00:21:22.551 --> 00:21:26.180
So to me, having been able to
have a foreign at Stanford but

00:21:26.180 --> 00:21:30.790
spending a lot of time with the community
and investing in Latin America.

00:21:30.790 --> 00:21:34.851
And looking at what was going on in
Brazil, in Colombia, and Argentina, and

00:21:34.851 --> 00:21:38.738
other countries was a really great
opportunity, so I'm glad I did that.

00:21:38.738 --> 00:21:45.112
&gt;&gt; You guys in combined,
you've led companies that in aggregate

00:21:45.112 --> 00:21:50.772
are worth more than $100
billion in Latin America.

00:21:50.772 --> 00:21:53.792
You all came to Stanford, you all started,

00:21:53.792 --> 00:21:58.210
you all decided to go back to Lat Am and
