being responsible with it.

00:33:40.282 --> 00:33:44.685
You generally do see businesses
that have good business model,

00:33:44.685 --> 00:33:50.077
real unit economics, real business
fundamentals since the very beginning.

00:33:50.077 --> 00:33:54.951
You still haven't really seen a lot
of the craziness that you sometimes,

00:33:54.951 --> 00:33:57.478
I think, you see in the US and Europe.

00:33:57.478 --> 00:34:03.058
I would say still, it's becoming more
appropriate, but it hasn't gotten to

00:34:03.058 --> 00:34:08.482
the point where it's absurd or people
are wasting money all over the place.

00:34:08.482 --> 00:34:14.185
That leaves as my two
cents on that perspective.

00:34:14.185 --> 00:34:16.766
&gt;&gt; I would add that it was hard for

00:34:16.766 --> 00:34:22.250
me in the beginning to envision
that we would build companies.

00:34:22.250 --> 00:34:26.208
They scaled up through, you're seeing
some of the companies here and

00:34:26.208 --> 00:34:29.173
the speeds that we're
seeing this companies grow.

00:34:29.173 --> 00:34:33.269
And is very good to see some
examples that that's possible and

00:34:33.269 --> 00:34:38.470
you see as robust companies with strong
talent platforms, strong cultures,

00:34:38.470 --> 00:34:43.460
and they really know how to develop
products that will solve big problems.

00:34:43.460 --> 00:34:50.134
And then I think what these sayings
is just a follow up to that.

00:34:50.134 --> 00:34:55.237
But the challenge for
myself that sometimes we struggle here is,

00:34:55.237 --> 00:34:59.897
as Marcus was saying,
it's very unpredictable in Latin.

00:34:59.897 --> 00:35:04.377
And so on the one hand, it's very
good to put money in the bank, and

00:35:04.377 --> 00:35:09.657
then comparing to a few years ago where
you didn't have the money in the bank and

00:35:09.657 --> 00:35:11.181
you had to keep going.

00:35:11.181 --> 00:35:15.378
So it's much better to have
the money in the bank, but

00:35:15.378 --> 00:35:19.109
you don't know if this wave
is going to continue or

00:35:19.109 --> 00:35:23.139
just be a short wave of
capital that comes and goes.

00:35:23.139 --> 00:35:28.064
And then this push and break,
it's kind of hard in the companies.

00:35:28.064 --> 00:35:31.312
And if we could have
more predictability and

00:35:31.312 --> 00:35:35.807
more stability of this capital and
you could plan around it.

00:35:35.807 --> 00:35:40.809
It would be much healthier for the
companies, I think to agree that I need

00:35:40.809 --> 00:35:45.483
to raise now, then raise a bunch of
cattle may be more than you need,

00:35:45.483 --> 00:35:49.013
they got the evaluation
the higher than it should.

00:35:49.013 --> 00:35:53.064
And you spend that in a way that
maybe a year or two from now,

00:35:53.064 --> 00:35:58.784
you want to sustain the same rhythm, and
then you have to pull back in the company.

00:35:58.784 --> 00:36:02.169
So that's kind of one of
the hardest things in Latin,

00:36:02.169 --> 00:36:04.133
I've seen the last two years.

00:36:04.133 --> 00:36:07.401
For example, every quarter or
every semester,

00:36:07.401 --> 00:36:12.516
we had to adjust our plan due to external
factors in a way that was like, man.

00:36:12.516 --> 00:36:17.382
[LAUGH] I know that's the dynamic business
and we are just seeing all the time.

00:36:17.382 --> 00:36:22.213
But in Latin, it's kind of a bit more
exciting and I think funding coming and

00:36:22.213 --> 00:36:26.007
going is one of the biggest reasons for
that.

00:36:26.007 --> 00:36:31.392
&gt;&gt; Sometimes I think the hype
if where there is hyper-reality,

00:36:31.392 --> 00:36:36.787
I think sometimes, you don't know,
until things unravel.

00:36:36.787 --> 00:36:41.692
But I'm not thinking about the future but
about the past.

00:36:41.692 --> 00:36:45.873
I compare the headlines of today and
they may seem somewhat exuberant

00:36:45.873 --> 00:36:49.418
to the headlines of approximately 12,
15 years ago.

00:36:49.418 --> 00:36:54.269
And the kinds of companies that were
raising money were companies that were

00:36:54.269 --> 00:36:58.196
doing oil exploration in
the Columbian Amazonian basin, or

00:36:58.196 --> 00:37:02.135
in the in Brazil, and
that was the exuberance then.

00:37:02.135 --> 00:37:06.524
And I'd rather much have the kind
of money that is flowing today into

00:37:06.524 --> 00:37:10.993
the region flow into the kind of
businesses that it's flowing into.

00:37:10.993 --> 00:37:15.889
And that there be a bubble there,
bubbles always tend to correct.

00:37:15.889 --> 00:37:20.529
Then in the kind of sectors that
we were seeing it flowing into,

00:37:20.529 --> 00:37:26.322
not about a decade ago, so I'd rather
much have this kind of market dynamics.

00:37:26.322 --> 00:37:27.767
This kind of destination for

00:37:27.767 --> 00:37:30.783
money than the ones that
the region saw in previous cycles.

00:37:35.522 --> 00:37:39.900
&gt;&gt; And I would add that beyond the money,
I'm very, very excited of what I'm seeing,

00:37:39.900 --> 00:37:41.825
because I'm seeing a lot of talent.

00:37:41.825 --> 00:37:44.597
There are a lot of people now
that see entrepreneurship,

00:37:44.597 --> 00:37:47.947
that really want to take on a problem and
solve it and they're seeing,

00:37:47.947 --> 00:37:50.802
with a capital flow that it's
something that can be done.

00:37:50.802 --> 00:37:54.009
So I think it's just going to be a lot
more stories of entrepreneurship from

00:37:54.009 --> 00:37:56.126
Latin America,
people doing amazing things.

00:37:56.126 --> 00:38:01.224
Because I'm seeing that people that want
to become entrepreneurs, that want to join

00:38:01.224 --> 00:38:06.491
an entrepreneurial team, which is amazing,
and we didn't have that five years ago.

00:38:06.491 --> 00:38:09.381
&gt;&gt; Loreanne, there's a question that came

00:38:09.381 --> 00:38:13.853
up a couple of times around you're
the only woman in this panel.

00:38:13.853 --> 00:38:19.325
How do you see that Latin American
startup environment evolving for

00:38:19.325 --> 00:38:21.251
more gender equality?

00:38:21.251 --> 00:38:25.570
&gt;&gt; Usually, I'm the only woman in the
panel, unless it's an only women panel,

00:38:25.570 --> 00:38:27.015
which there are not a lot.

00:38:27.015 --> 00:38:28.484
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Well, there's still a lot to do.

00:38:28.484 --> 00:38:33.516
There are a lot of social things going on
in Latin America that's been going on for

00:38:33.516 --> 00:38:37.668
a while, in terms of what's
the women's role and how we could do.

00:38:37.668 --> 00:38:42.391
So we're not there yet, we still need
to figure out a way to get more women

00:38:42.391 --> 00:38:45.110
interested in becoming entrepreneurs.

00:38:45.110 --> 00:38:49.292
Get more women interested not only
in becoming entrepreneurs but

00:38:49.292 --> 00:38:51.198
in solving bigger problems.

00:38:51.198 --> 00:38:52.679
Because as women need,

00:38:52.679 --> 00:38:57.279
we usually tend to go to smaller markets,
we need to go to bigger markets.

00:38:57.279 --> 00:39:01.927
And also to trust ourselves more
that we that we're going to do it.

00:39:01.927 --> 00:39:04.974
So it's going to take a little bit, but

00:39:04.974 --> 00:39:09.103
I know we're a few women
here as entrepreneurs and

00:39:09.103 --> 00:39:14.527
we're really trying to see how we
can have a multiplying effect.

00:39:16.246 --> 00:39:20.504
&gt;&gt; That means you have Christina
as a co-founder in new bank,

00:39:20.504 --> 00:39:25.100
as you think about, as in you see
a lot of people that are starting

00:39:25.100 --> 00:39:29.631
companies in Brazil,
are you seeing any other changes there?

00:39:32.398 --> 00:39:34.398
&gt;&gt; Yeah, I think I've seen changes.

00:39:34.398 --> 00:39:39.839
I don't think it's as fast as we all wish.

00:39:39.839 --> 00:39:44.130
I think Chris, my co-founder,
is still one of the few

00:39:44.130 --> 00:39:49.075
Brazilian co-founder,
one of the large tech companies, and

00:39:49.075 --> 00:39:55.255
that's very surprising in a country
where over 50% population are women.

00:39:55.255 --> 00:39:59.238
But I see it more and more,
it's happening more and more.

00:39:59.238 --> 00:40:04.940
There is not only More women going
in starting their own businesses,

00:40:04.940 --> 00:40:09.440
but also venture capital focused on
supporting women led businesses.

00:40:09.440 --> 00:40:14.413
And I think just the entire ecosystem is
arranging itself to make it much more

00:40:14.413 --> 00:40:16.210
inclusive and available.

00:40:16.210 --> 00:40:18.820
So I think it's too early.

00:40:18.820 --> 00:40:22.600
It's definitely not where it should be but
it's the trend is good.

00:40:26.140 --> 00:40:32.177
&gt;&gt; One question that comes a lot here
is how are you recruiting people for

00:40:32.177 --> 00:40:33.747
your companies?

00:40:33.747 --> 00:40:38.835
What language is the official
language companies are speaking when

00:40:38.835 --> 00:40:43.574
you hire technologists and
developers in different regions.

00:40:43.574 --> 00:40:48.349
As Latin America becomes as all of you
are growing to multiple countries,

00:40:48.349 --> 00:40:51.672
how is that an issue and
opportunity in the future?

00:40:56.235 --> 00:40:58.200
So let me put this way.

00:40:58.200 --> 00:41:02.730
Marcus, what's your official language in
Mercado Libre, in the technology team.

00:41:04.880 --> 00:41:08.656
&gt;&gt; Meaning coding language or
&gt;&gt; No

00:41:08.656 --> 00:41:09.890
&gt;&gt; They speaking language,

00:41:09.890 --> 00:41:12.194
when you have people
from different countries.

00:41:12.194 --> 00:41:15.099
&gt;&gt; It's Spanish,
typically Spanish historically,

00:41:15.099 --> 00:41:18.620
would I speak to Stereo we'll
communicate in English?

00:41:18.620 --> 00:41:23.176
And the official language or
the presentation Every material that is

00:41:23.176 --> 00:41:26.670
written in the company
is written in English.

00:41:26.670 --> 00:41:30.779
So our board operates in English,
we present to our investors in English so

00:41:30.779 --> 00:41:34.562
we present to ourselves even though
we might be speaking in Spanish,

00:41:34.562 --> 00:41:37.800
the written material,
everything is in English.

00:41:37.800 --> 00:41:44.301
And, Brazil is 60% of our revenues.

00:41:44.301 --> 00:41:48.754
But most of the people speak
Spanish in the company, but

00:41:48.754 --> 00:41:54.345
it's kind of out of respect to
the Brazilians we use a neutral language

00:41:54.345 --> 00:42:00.909
which is English oftentimes when we are
communicating with our Brazilian teams.

00:42:00.909 --> 00:42:03.965
&gt;&gt; Havid.
&gt;&gt; We started with English first,

00:42:03.965 --> 00:42:08.145
we had a team where one of
my co founders is American,

00:42:08.145 --> 00:42:12.650
dear co founder is Brazilian,
I'm Colombian.

00:42:12.650 --> 00:42:17.526
So English ended up being the default in
our team of the first ten ,15 employees

00:42:17.526 --> 00:42:22.329
that were already a number of people from
the US who didn't speak any Spanish and

00:42:22.329 --> 00:42:23.206
Portuguese.

00:42:23.206 --> 00:42:28.961
So English became the kind of
the Lingua Franca of the entire company.

00:42:28.961 --> 00:42:33.617
That has been an opportunity as well as
a challenge I think opportunity is that as

00:42:33.617 --> 00:42:36.535
we go now globally expand
where we're recruiting

00:42:36.535 --> 00:42:40.513
talent we already have people
from 45 different nationalities.

00:42:40.513 --> 00:42:45.620
We're very spread around that is already
a culture that everything is in English.

00:42:45.620 --> 00:42:49.115
Although communication is in English,
people can get up to speak quickly and

00:42:49.115 --> 00:42:50.889
what's going on, and it's easier for

00:42:50.889 --> 00:42:54.630
people from around the world to to
get integrated with different teams.

00:42:54.630 --> 00:42:57.733
The challenge to that note is that
especially in the earliest stages in some

00:42:57.733 --> 00:43:00.990
of the countries,
a lot of people don't speak English.

00:43:00.990 --> 00:43:03.191
You might become a very
exclusionary company,

00:43:03.191 --> 00:43:06.656
if you require English as a requirement
to enter you might be leaving a lot of

00:43:06.656 --> 00:43:10.850
really important talent that just doesn't
speak English didn't have the opportunity.

00:43:10.850 --> 00:43:14.980
So, the challenge is how do
you provide English lessons?

00:43:14.980 --> 00:43:19.455
How do you have inclusive HR process of
recruiting processes to allow to recruit

00:43:19.455 --> 00:43:24.150
people, sometimes from lower income
segments that don't speak English?

00:43:24.150 --> 00:43:29.090
Train them in English and make them
able to really fit within the company.

00:43:29.090 --> 00:43:31.799
That's one of the biggest challenge
that we're working right now

00:43:31.799 --> 00:43:34.747
because I don't think the answer also
is we're an English company, and

00:43:34.747 --> 00:43:37.350
all Latin Americans need to
figure out how to speak English.

00:43:37.350 --> 00:43:39.650
At the end of the day we
are a Latin American company by itself.

00:43:40.660 --> 00:43:43.221
So the challenge is finding that mix and

00:43:43.221 --> 00:43:48.660
ultimately is about the Spanish people
have to spot learn Portuguese and English.

00:43:48.660 --> 00:43:53.660
The Portuguese people have to
speak Spanish and English and

00:43:53.660 --> 00:43:58.560
try to get everybody to speak
all the languages ultimately

00:43:58.560 --> 00:44:02.972
working some kind of mixture that so
far is working.

00:44:02.972 --> 00:44:06.206
&gt;&gt; Gabriel N Kinto and Err.

00:44:06.206 --> 00:44:11.016
&gt;&gt; Yeah, I think it's about the same
thing we're still not operating in

00:44:11.016 --> 00:44:12.330
other countries.

00:44:12.330 --> 00:44:18.750
But, since the beginning we also have
an internal documentation English.

00:44:18.750 --> 00:44:23.748
And sometimes we have the opportunity
to bring people from from outside

00:44:23.748 --> 00:44:26.759
even before the remote world phenomenal.

00:44:26.759 --> 00:44:31.324
And I think that's an opportunity,
it's the challenge for

00:44:31.324 --> 00:44:33.770
everybody to adapt to that.

00:44:33.770 --> 00:44:35.910
But as David and other said here,

00:44:35.910 --> 00:44:39.500
I think is also the opportunity
to force us to do that.

00:44:39.500 --> 00:44:44.531
So if you have a chance of working
a company that will allow you and

00:44:44.531 --> 00:44:48.365
force you, and
require you to develop English.

00:44:48.365 --> 00:44:52.830
It's just better than going to school and
not practicing it outside.

00:44:52.830 --> 00:44:57.070
So I think it's a forcing mechanism and
is inevitable.

00:44:58.390 --> 00:45:01.437
So, nothing very different to add

00:45:01.437 --> 00:45:07.929
there
&gt;&gt; Well it feels

00:45:07.929 --> 00:45:12.555
that we have the common learning here
is English is an important language for

00:45:12.555 --> 00:45:16.966
presentations and for recruiting,
challenging, getting out more and

00:45:16.966 --> 00:45:22.400
more people as companies grow to speak
the language or to get the bases running.

00:45:22.400 --> 00:45:27.016
But it is something that I will tell you
was not the case 15 20 years ago, so

00:45:27.016 --> 00:45:31.610
it's changing in the region and
it's changing really fast.

00:45:31.610 --> 00:45:36.440
We're going to transition, there's one
question that I'm going to answer but as

00:45:36.440 --> 00:45:41.280
we transition to having the Stanford team
give a little bit of some data on GSB.

00:45:42.900 --> 00:45:47.124
There's a question here that says I feel
Venezuela is a different case from most of

00:45:47.124 --> 00:45:49.760
the region given its
current economic condition.

00:45:49.760 --> 00:45:53.897
What would you recommend to an aspiring
MBA student who would like to eventually

00:45:53.897 --> 00:45:57.120
come back to his country and
make a difference?

00:45:57.120 --> 00:46:00.762
So I'm going to take a stab at answering
that question guys feel free to jump in if

00:46:00.762 --> 00:46:02.040
you want to.

00:46:02.040 --> 00:46:06.844
But to that question, this is part of
the reason why I started sisapuelle

00:46:06.844 --> 00:46:10.570
which you're going to
hear in a couple minutes.

00:46:10.570 --> 00:46:13.390
A little bit more about
from this team at Stanford.

00:46:13.390 --> 00:46:20.670
We're basically creating this program
that allows any Latin American to apply.

00:46:20.670 --> 00:46:23.994
Any Latin American that has
certain financial conditions

00:46:23.994 --> 00:46:28.343
below a certain salary range to apply for
free to Stanford Business School.

00:46:28.343 --> 00:46:32.745
And once you get admitted and
you get accepted,

00:46:32.745 --> 00:46:38.469
there's a program where we will
sponsor have a fellowship and

00:46:38.469 --> 00:46:44.743
have a few students per year that
will get scholarships to attend and

00:46:44.743 --> 00:46:49.168
get most of his tuition paid for
by Sisapuelle.

00:46:49.168 --> 00:46:53.779
With the purpose that if you go back to
the region after studying and you end up

00:46:53.779 --> 00:46:58.535
having an impact and in you're not going
back to the country that you are from,

00:46:58.535 --> 00:47:02.030
we will waive any other
that you have remaining.

00:47:02.030 --> 00:47:06.992
So the idea is to get more people
to attend school in the US get

00:47:06.992 --> 00:47:11.855
prepared with the way Marcos,
Laurie, Dahveed, Dave,

00:47:11.855 --> 00:47:18.107
Halomeen had a chance to be exposed,
go back to the region have an impact and

00:47:18.107 --> 00:47:22.060
do it in a way that is cost efficient for
you.

00:47:22.060 --> 00:47:23.870
And that's part of what we
want to do as Sisapuele.

00:47:23.870 --> 00:47:25.780
And in Venezuela in particular.

00:47:25.780 --> 00:47:27.320
I hear you I'm from Venezuela.

00:47:27.320 --> 00:47:29.180
It's hard to go back now.

00:47:29.180 --> 00:47:31.649
But you can definitely go
back to the region and

00:47:31.649 --> 00:47:35.401
we'll cross our fingers that when
it gets better, then we can enter.

00:47:35.401 --> 00:47:39.431
This generation of people that went to
this business schools that build these

00:47:39.431 --> 00:47:43.990
networks of relationships, will have
the impact to change an entire country.

00:47:43.990 --> 00:47:48.796
The same way all of you are changing
your country's by bringing better

00:47:48.796 --> 00:47:53.277
financial services, better e-commerce,
better ways to buy or

00:47:53.277 --> 00:47:57.105
finance a car, better ways to buy or
finance your home or

00:47:57.105 --> 00:48:02.300
finance or finance a rental these
are all things that impact society.

00:48:02.300 --> 00:48:07.127
And funny enough we got an amazing group
that went to the school and had and

00:48:07.127 --> 00:48:08.725
had a chance to do this.

00:48:08.725 --> 00:48:11.665
So with that I want to
transition quickly to

00:48:11.665 --> 00:48:16.035
the Stanford team to see if they
have anything they want to add.

00:48:16.035 --> 00:48:20.006
&gt;&gt; Thank you, Mickey, and thank you so
much to Mickey and our panelists.

00:48:20.006 --> 00:48:24.842
This was just fantastic and the time
flew by, I feel like I could listen to

00:48:24.842 --> 00:48:28.432
your insights for a long time,
so much appreciated.

00:48:28.432 --> 00:48:33.011
And Mickey thank you so much for teeing
up the information about Si se puede.

00:48:33.011 --> 00:48:37.231
As Mickey mentioned, and
thanks to his generous support we are so

00:48:37.231 --> 00:48:41.990
excited to offer this program at the GSB
specifically to those who are from

00:48:41.990 --> 00:48:47.056
Latin America who meet, as Mickey
mentioned, a certain financial condition

00:48:47.056 --> 00:48:51.614
that's making less than US $40,000
when you apply to the GSB.

00:48:51.614 --> 00:48:56.461
If you meet these conditions,
your application fee will be waived.

00:48:56.461 --> 00:48:59.453
So this is not something
you need to apply for,

00:48:59.453 --> 00:49:02.305
there's no additional paperwork needed.

00:49:02.305 --> 00:49:07.911
This will just happen in your application
if you meet these conditions.

00:49:07.911 --> 00:49:09.961
We're so excited to be offering this.

00:49:09.961 --> 00:49:12.489
So for those of you who are on today,
thank you for joining us so

00:49:12.489 --> 00:49:13.571
you can learn about this.

00:49:13.571 --> 00:49:15.573
And for
those who are just hearing about it,

00:49:15.573 --> 00:49:17.694
feel free to share this
with your friends too.

00:49:17.694 --> 00:49:19.842
I did see a question in the chat.

00:49:19.842 --> 00:49:22.536
Just, is this the same as Knight Hennessy.

00:49:22.536 --> 00:49:23.746
And that's a great question.

00:49:23.746 --> 00:49:27.782
As Mickey mentioned, not only are we
trying to make it possible for

00:49:27.782 --> 00:49:30.666
you to apply with this
application fee waiver,

00:49:30.666 --> 00:49:34.867
but we want to enable you to be able
to attend also, should you get in.

00:49:34.867 --> 00:49:39.008
So Mickey mentioned the fellowship
money that is available,

00:49:39.008 --> 00:49:42.838
once you are admitted,
our financial aid is need based.

00:49:42.838 --> 00:49:47.291
So should you have a high level of need
there are fellowships available to

00:49:47.291 --> 00:49:48.975
help you be able to attend.

00:49:48.975 --> 00:49:52.602
And one of the questions that was out
there is this the same as Knight Hennessy?

00:49:52.602 --> 00:49:56.200
I want to point out that there
are fellowships specifically to support

00:49:56.200 --> 00:49:57.812
students from Latin America,

00:49:57.812 --> 00:50:01.793
but there are also many other fellowship
programs such as Knight Hennessy.

00:50:01.793 --> 00:50:06.709
That's another way for
you to apply to that program separately.

00:50:06.709 --> 00:50:10.972
You would have the potential to be
a knight Hennessy fellow as well

00:50:10.972 --> 00:50:12.766
as a Stanford GSB student.

00:50:12.766 --> 00:50:16.916
So Si se puede and
Knight Hennessy are separate programs.

00:50:16.916 --> 00:50:21.092
But they are indicative of the level
of support that's out there for

00:50:21.092 --> 00:50:23.080
you to be able to attend the GSB.

00:50:23.080 --> 00:50:26.313
There are millions of dollars
of fellowships awarded,

00:50:26.313 --> 00:50:31.404
our average fellowship each year per
student is around 40,000 that's average,

00:50:31.404 --> 00:50:33.897
meaning many students get more than that.

00:50:33.897 --> 00:50:36.795
And so whatever year you
are thinking about applying,

00:50:36.795 --> 00:50:40.395
I encourage you to seek out what
financial programs are available.

00:50:40.395 --> 00:50:46.234
But right now, Si se puede is available as
an application fee waiver and fellowships

00:50:46.234 --> 00:50:51.346
should you be able to attend, in
addition to many other programs we have.

00:50:51.346 --> 00:50:56.698
So I did see a few other questions in
the chat that were admissions focused.

00:50:56.698 --> 00:51:02.022
So I encourage you all to check our events
page if you are interested in attending

00:51:02.022 --> 00:51:06.722
an information session that's more
about the application process.

00:51:06.722 --> 00:51:11.588
And we'd love to have you there and help
you learn more about applying to the GSB.

00:51:11.588 --> 00:51:14.892
I know we're a few minutes over, so
again, I just want to wrap up in

00:51:14.892 --> 00:51:18.677
thanking Mickey you're so much for
not only his support of this program but

00:51:18.677 --> 00:51:21.166
for helping bring this
dynamic panel together.

00:51:21.166 --> 00:51:23.135
It was such a great event.

00:51:23.135 --> 00:51:25.503
So great to hear all of your insights.

00:51:25.503 --> 00:51:27.720
I thank you all for your time.

00:51:27.720 --> 00:51:30.511
And again,
thank you to everyone who attended today.

00:51:30.511 --> 00:51:32.866
It was great to have you with us and

00:51:32.866 --> 00:51:36.244
I hope you found this as
inspirational as I did.

00:51:36.244 --> 00:51:38.617
&gt;&gt; One last question to
finish to the panelists.

00:51:38.617 --> 00:51:40.520
Who was the best student?

00:51:40.520 --> 00:51:45.261
